WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.940
It's easy enough to say I'm going to
start learning this language, but I know

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.650
from experience, it's a lot of work.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.210
Hi there's Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:10.830
Today I want to sort of ask
myself a question: why do you

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.450
want to learn another language?

00:00:13.425 --> 00:00:17.625
Um, and, um, but first, before I
get into the subject, uh, if you

00:00:17.625 --> 00:00:21.494
enjoy my videos, please subscribe,
click on the bell for notifications.

00:00:22.035 --> 00:00:26.085
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:26.775 --> 00:00:30.705
So why has this subject
suddenly come to my mind?

00:00:30.735 --> 00:00:35.775
Well, my, a large part of my inspiration
for learning languages comes from

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:41.760
different series that my wife watches on
Netflix and I kind of go in and out of the

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.220
TV room and sometimes sit down and watch.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:50.630
And she was watching an
Indian movie ... or something.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:55.470
Uh, and of course it's, it's
in Bombay it's a hundred years

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.420
ago or whenever, 80 years ago.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:02.160
And it's very exotic and all of these
things that sort of, you know, and

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.660
of course I pick up the odd word in
Hindi that sounds a bit like Persian.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.270
Uh, the odd one and it then becomes
very tempting to say, you know what,

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:17.040
I should learn Hindi or Punjabi
or one of those Indian languages.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.280
And so then I say to myself,
well, here you are, you've been

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:25.620
struggling with Persian and Arabic
and of course neglecting other

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.240
languages that you've learned.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.240
Why would you take on
yet another language?

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.750
And, um, yeah, this is
an interesting question.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:39.360
Obviously, the more languages you
have or the more languages you try to

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.280
learn, uh, the less well you're going
to speak in languages that you have

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:49.180
already spent time trying to learn.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:53.010
You know, I sometimes say to
myself, why did you ever bother

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:56.220
trying to learn Persian and Arabic?

00:01:56.370 --> 00:02:00.900
When, you know, I, maybe you should
focus on Korean, which is not

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:02.790
at a level that you would like.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:09.750
Um, and, and the, the simple answer
is that I'm always curious, you

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:15.060
know, as imperfect as my Arabic and
Persian are, and I will perhaps do

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:20.175
a um, maybe a conversation with my
tutor again, in those languages, and

00:02:20.175 --> 00:02:21.675
you'll see that it's full of holes.

00:02:21.855 --> 00:02:25.095
And yet it's at a level
where I enjoy them.

00:02:25.875 --> 00:02:33.855
Uh, I enjoy listening to my, um, you know,
Radio Farda uh, podcast news in Farsi.

00:02:33.855 --> 00:02:35.265
I understand a fair amount of it.

00:02:35.805 --> 00:02:38.025
I enjoy, for example, my Jordanian.

00:02:39.135 --> 00:02:41.445
Um, cartoon series.

00:02:41.535 --> 00:02:45.765
So I'm enjoying participating to
some extent in that language, in

00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:47.804
that culture, however imperfectly.

00:02:48.525 --> 00:02:52.424
So whatever I put in, I feel I get back.

00:02:53.055 --> 00:03:02.010
Now, how exciting would it be to have some
sense of how some Indian language works?

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.730
Uh, probably Hindi because
it's the most widely spoken.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:11.460
And of course the Bollywood movies are
in Hindi, but here in Vancouver, most

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:16.490
of the people from India are in fact
from the Punjab so they speak Punjabi.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:22.215
Um, probably I have no idea how mutually
intelligible those languages are,

00:03:22.215 --> 00:03:25.125
but, um, it would be fun to do them.

00:03:25.455 --> 00:03:26.985
And so it becomes fun.

00:03:28.085 --> 00:03:31.605
Uh it's it's a challenge, like part
of learning another languages simply

00:03:31.605 --> 00:03:33.105
because the language is there.

00:03:33.495 --> 00:03:35.385
So it's like, why do you climb a mountain?

00:03:35.385 --> 00:03:37.005
Because the mountain is there.

00:03:37.455 --> 00:03:41.775
You know, uh, I look back to when I
wrote my book The Linguist on Language,

00:03:42.525 --> 00:03:48.060
uh, I guess I was 57 or so and I
had nine languages and I had enjoyed

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:49.530
every one of those nine languages.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.010
Well, now I'm approaching
my 20th language.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:04:01.275
Um, mostly since the age of 60 and it's
brought me so much and I can think of the

00:04:01.275 --> 00:04:04.905
Egyptian movies that I watched training
very hard to understand something,

00:04:04.905 --> 00:04:09.135
not understanding too much, but, but
because of my exposure to that language

00:04:09.135 --> 00:04:13.995
and the few things that I do understand
I'm more involved, I, I can feel and

00:04:13.995 --> 00:04:16.155
smell and touch the culture more.

00:04:16.605 --> 00:04:22.755
Uh, similarly with Persian, where Sahara
in Iran has sent me some, uh, Irani

00:04:22.765 --> 00:04:27.165
movies and I'm confident, maybe more
confident in Persian than in Arabic,

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.960
that I'll eventually be on, be able
to understand those movies quite well.

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:34.050
Uh, so it, it brings me all of that.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:37.080
And I think I made the point in,
in a number of my other videos

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.330
that whatever I achieve is good.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:40.950
It's all a plus.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.250
There is no negative.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:46.335
Perhaps the only negative is that
I haven't spent that time working

00:04:46.335 --> 00:04:49.755
on some of my other languages,
but I can go back to them.

00:04:50.085 --> 00:04:52.455
Whatever I put into them is there.

00:04:52.485 --> 00:04:53.515
I'm not going to lose it.

00:04:53.515 --> 00:04:55.935
It might take me a little
while to refresh them.

00:04:56.415 --> 00:05:00.465
So I don't know, why
learn another language?

00:05:00.495 --> 00:05:04.385
I guess the question can be asked
of someone like me who has tried to

00:05:04.385 --> 00:05:10.155
learn, say, 20 languages and who might
look at trying to learn about another,

00:05:10.185 --> 00:05:14.565
yet another language or two or three
or someone who is still struggling

00:05:14.565 --> 00:05:16.245
with their first foreign language.

00:05:16.515 --> 00:05:19.685
And of course, for many
people, it's not a hobby.

00:05:19.925 --> 00:05:23.195
For many people, it's, it's,
there's a practical need.

00:05:23.555 --> 00:05:27.005
Uh, they might be an immigrant or a
refugee in a country, in Germany or

00:05:27.005 --> 00:05:30.005
in North America or somewhere they
have to learn the local language.

00:05:30.575 --> 00:05:35.285
Or, uh, if you're living somewhere
where you do business with people

00:05:35.285 --> 00:05:38.695
from another country and you need that
language, then of course there's a

00:05:38.695 --> 00:05:42.875
greater sort of need for the language,
but I'm not in that situation.

00:05:42.965 --> 00:05:46.085
I'm in the situation where I
learn languages for pleasure.

00:05:46.745 --> 00:05:51.755
And uh, why would I take on yet another
language with all of an, and of course

00:05:52.025 --> 00:05:54.995
it's easy enough to say I'm going to
start learning this language, but I know

00:05:54.995 --> 00:05:56.765
from experience, it's a lot of work.

00:05:57.065 --> 00:06:02.585
So once I commit myself to sort of
spending time with that language, it's

00:06:02.585 --> 00:06:10.225
going to be another sort of times sump,
but it's an enjoyable pursuit for me.

00:06:10.725 --> 00:06:15.135
Uh, and not only is the actual study of
the language enjoyable, but everything

00:06:15.135 --> 00:06:18.224
that comes with it, the discovery
of the culture, getting closer to

00:06:18.224 --> 00:06:24.484
those people and seeing those people
less sort of in a caricature form or

00:06:24.484 --> 00:06:30.255
stereotype form, but rather seeing
them as they are and hearing how they

00:06:30.434 --> 00:06:32.325
speak and how they express themselves.

00:06:32.655 --> 00:06:38.810
So that's why I continue to add
languages, although I don't achieve a

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:45.859
high level of fluency I do achieve a
very worthwhile level of familiarity

00:06:45.859 --> 00:06:50.120
with those languages and that's why
I would continue learning languages.

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:53.750
And I would like to suggest you
have a look at two languages or two

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:55.280
videos that I did on the subject.

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:59.360
Uh, one was six months ago where I
talk about learning two languages at

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:03.260
once and another one, three months
ago, where I would talk about learning

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:04.880
multiple languages at the same time.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:10.560
And that might give you a different
perspective on the issue of taking on,

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:17.250
uh, different languages or continuing
to challenge yourself with more

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:19.830
languages when you already know some.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:22.290
And I'm sure I'm not the
only one in this situation.

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:23.880
So I look forward to hearing from you.

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:25.170
Bye for now.

